# Line endings are a property of this repository, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this file, what lands in a working tree depends on each machine's core.autocrlf: the
# checkout is CRLF on Windows and LF on CI, and anything that reads the tree as text can mean two
# different things in the two places. That is not hypothetical here - a source-scanning test
# anchored a pattern with $, which matches before the \n of a CRLF pair, and so ran a weaker check
# on Windows than the one CI ran (SourceTree.Read now normalises, which is the other half).
#
# text=auto: git decides text from content and stores it LF-normalised. Every blob here is already
# LF, so this pins what is true rather than changing anything.
#
# The working tree's own form is left to core.autocrlf, so a Windows checkout stays CRLF. Forcing
# eol=lf here was tried and backed out: it makes git report every already-checked-out file as
# needing an update on a machine with core.autocrlf=true, which is noise with nothing behind it.
# Nothing should depend on the working-tree form anyway - SourceTree.Read normalises what the
# architecture tests read, which is what made the difference matter once.
* text=auto

# Files a shell or a Windows host reads directly, where the ending is not cosmetic. None are tracked
# today; they are named so the first one added does not have to rediscover this.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Content git must never rewrite. Declared ahead of need for the same reason as above: a mangled
# binary is discovered late and looks like corruption rather than a checkout rule.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.snk binary
*.pfx binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
